\subsection{Practical Controller Forms}

Not all applications require all three terms. Common reduced-order controllers include:

\textbf{P Controller:} $C(s) = \Kp$
\begin{itemize}
    \item Simplest form; used when steady-state error is acceptable
    \item Applications: non-critical level control, manual-like operation
\end{itemize}

\textbf{PI Controller:} $C(s) = \Kp + \dfrac{\Ki}{s} = \Kp\left(1 + \dfrac{1}{\Ti s}\right)$
\begin{itemize}
    \item Most common industrial controller
    \item Eliminates steady-state error without noise amplification
    \item Applications: flow control, pressure control, slow temperature loops
\end{itemize}

\textbf{PD Controller:} $C(s) = \Kp + \Kd s = \Kp(1 + \Td s)$
\begin{itemize}
    \item Improves transient response without affecting steady-state
    \item Rarely used alone due to steady-state error
    \item Applications: systems where overshoot is critical and error is tolerable
\end{itemize}

\textbf{PID Controller:} Full three-term controller
\begin{itemize}
    \item Used when both steady-state accuracy and fast transient response are required
    \item Applications: precise temperature control, position control, process control
\end{itemize}


%=============================================================================
\section{Practical Experiment: Temperature Control System}
%=============================================================================

Theory comes alive through hands-on experimentation. In this section, we design, build, and tune a PID temperature controller using readily available components. This experiment demonstrates the progressive improvement from P-only to PI to full PID control.

\subsection{System Overview}

Our experimental setup controls the temperature of a small thermal mass (an aluminum block or water bath) using a resistive heating element. The system components are:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Microcontroller}: Arduino Uno (or compatible)
    \item \textbf{Temperature sensor}: 10k$\Omega$ NTC thermistor (or TMP36 analog sensor)
    \item \textbf{Heating element}: 10$\Omega$, 10W power resistor
    \item \textbf{Driver}: N-channel MOSFET (e.g., IRF520) for PWM control
    \item \textbf{Thermal mass}: Small aluminum block (approximately $30 \times 30 \times 10$ mm)
    \item \textbf{Power supply}: 12V, 2A DC supply
\end{itemize}

