\subsection{Software Implementation}

The following Arduino code implements P, PI, and PID control with selectable modes:

\begin{lstlisting}[style=arduino, caption={Arduino PID Temperature Controller}]
// PID Temperature Controller
// Demonstrates P, PI, and PID control modes

// Pin definitions
const int THERMISTOR_PIN = A0;
const int HEATER_PIN = 9;

// Thermistor parameters (10k NTC, B=3950)
const float SERIES_RESISTOR = 10000.0;
const float NOMINAL_RESISTANCE = 10000.0;
const float NOMINAL_TEMP = 25.0;
const float B_COEFFICIENT = 3950.0;

// Control parameters
float Kp = 50.0;    // Proportional gain
float Ki = 0.5;     // Integral gain
float Kd = 10.0;    // Derivative gain
float setpoint = 50.0;  // Target temperature (C)

// Control mode: 0=P, 1=PI, 2=PID
int controlMode = 2;

// State variables
float integral = 0.0;
float previousError = 0.0;
unsigned long previousTime = 0;

// Anti-windup limit
const float INTEGRAL_MAX = 500.0;

void setup() {
  pinMode(HEATER_PIN, OUTPUT);
  Serial.begin(9600);
  previousTime = millis();

  Serial.println("Time(ms),Setpoint,Temperature,Output,Error");
}

float readTemperature() {
  int rawADC = analogRead(THERMISTOR_PIN);

  // Convert to resistance
  float resistance = SERIES_RESISTOR / (1023.0/rawADC - 1.0);

  // Steinhart-Hart equation (simplified B-parameter)
  float steinhart = resistance / NOMINAL_RESISTANCE;
  steinhart = log(steinhart) / B_COEFFICIENT;
  steinhart += 1.0 / (NOMINAL_TEMP + 273.15);
  float tempK = 1.0 / steinhart;

  return tempK - 273.15;  // Convert to Celsius
}

float computePID(float temperature) {
  unsigned long currentTime = millis();
  float dt = (currentTime - previousTime) / 1000.0;  // seconds

  if (dt <= 0) return 0;

  // Calculate error
  float error = setpoint - temperature;

  // Proportional term
  float P_term = Kp * error;

  // Integral term (with anti-windup)
  float I_term = 0;
  if (controlMode >= 1) {
    integral += error * dt;
    integral = constrain(integral, -INTEGRAL_MAX, INTEGRAL_MAX);
    I_term = Ki * integral;
  }

  // Derivative term
  float D_term = 0;
  if (controlMode >= 2) {
    float derivative = (error - previousError) / dt;
    D_term = Kd * derivative;
  }

  // Update state
  previousError = error;
  previousTime = currentTime;

  // Compute total output
  float output = P_term + I_term + D_term;

  // Clamp to valid PWM range
  output = constrain(output, 0, 255);

  return output;
}

void loop() {
  // Read temperature
  float temperature = readTemperature();

  // Compute control output
  float output = computePID(temperature);

  // Apply to heater
  analogWrite(HEATER_PIN, (int)output);

  // Log data for plotting
  Serial.print(millis());
  Serial.print(",");
  Serial.print(setpoint);
  Serial.print(",");
  Serial.print(temperature);
  Serial.print(",");
  Serial.print(output);
  Serial.print(",");
  Serial.println(setpoint - temperature);

  delay(100);  // 10 Hz control loop
}
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Experimental Procedure}

\textbf{Step 1: Characterize the Open-Loop System}

Before implementing control, understand your plant:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Apply a constant PWM (e.g., 128 = 50\% duty cycle)
    \item Record temperature versus time
    \item Identify the time constant $\tau$ (time to reach 63.2\% of final value)
    \item Identify the steady-state gain $K$ (temperature rise per unit PWM)
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Step 2: P-Only Control}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Set \texttt{controlMode = 0}
    \item Start with low $\Kp$ (e.g., 10) and increase gradually
    \item Observe the steady-state error (offset from setpoint)
    \item Note: Higher $\Kp$ reduces but never eliminates the offset
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Step 3: PI Control}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Set \texttt{controlMode = 1}
    \item Keep $\Kp$ from Step 2
    \item Start with small $\Ki$ (e.g., 0.1) and increase
    \item Observe the steady-state error approach zero
    \item Note: Too much $\Ki$ causes overshoot and oscillation
\end{enumerate}

\textbf{Step 4: PID Control}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item Set \texttt{controlMode = 2}
    \item Keep $\Kp$ and $\Ki$ from previous steps
    \item Start with small $\Kd$ (e.g., 5) and increase
    \item Observe reduced overshoot and faster settling
    \item Note: Too much $\Kd$ may cause high-frequency oscillation (noise sensitivity)
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Expected Results}

Figure~\ref{fig:experimental_results} shows typical experimental results from this setup.

